+# Fix: login stuck spinning after returning from the browser (Android)
+
+## Root cause
+
+`src-tauri/Cargo.toml` was updated from:
+
+```toml
+reqwest = { version = "0.12", features = ["json", "rustls-tls"], default-features = false }
+```
+to:
+```toml
+reqwest = { version = "0.13", features = ["json", "rustls"], default-features = false }
+```
+
+This isn't just a version bump — reqwest 0.13 renamed the `rustls` feature, and as
+part of that, changed what it verifies TLS certificates *with*:
+
+- reqwest 0.12's `rustls-tls` → bundled Mozilla root store (`webpki-roots`). No
+  OS/platform integration needed, works identically everywhere.
+- reqwest 0.13's `rustls` → `rustls-platform-verifier`, which verifies against
+  each OS's *native* trust store. On Android this requires an explicit one-time
+  JNI init call (`rustls_platform_verifier::android::init_with_env`) with a real
+  JVM `Env`/`Context`, made *before any HTTPS request*. Nothing in this codebase
+  makes that call, and it also needs a small Kotlin/Gradle component bundled
+  into `src-tauri/gen/android` that isn't there either.
+
+Result: the first HTTPS request made on Android panics:
+
+```
+thread 'tokio-rt-worker' panicked at rustls-platform-verifier-0.7.0/src/android.rs:90:10:
+Expect rustls-platform-verifier to be initialized
+```
+— confirmed directly in `log-new3.log`.
+
+That first HTTPS request is `AuthFlow::exchange_code()` in `src-tauri/src/auth.rs`,
+called the instant the `m6loapp://` deep link fires after the browser redirect —
+i.e. exactly the point where you see the spinner and nothing else happens.
+
+**Why a stuck spinner and not a crash:** `[profile.release] panic = "abort"` was
+present in 2.1.0 and is no longer in the current `Cargo.toml`. With the current
+default (`panic = "unwind"`), only the one panicking background task dies — the
+rest of the app, including the UI, keeps running. So the Tauri command's promise
+on the frontend side simply never resolves. (I did *not* restore `panic = "abort"`
+— see "Also noticed" below.)
+
+## The fix
+
+New file `src-tauri/src/tls.rs` rebuilds the exact bundled-root TLS behavior
+2.1.0 had (verified by downloading and reading the actual reqwest 0.13.4,
+rustls 0.23.43, and webpki-roots 1.0.9 sources from crates.io), and hands it to
+`reqwest` via `tls_backend_preconfigured()` instead of letting `reqwest` build
+its own platform-verifier-based config:
+
+```rust
+pub fn client_builder() -> reqwest::ClientBuilder {
+    reqwest::Client::builder().tls_backend_preconfigured(tls_config())
+}
+
+pub fn new_client() -> reqwest::Client {
+    client_builder().build().expect(...)
+}
+```
+
+Every `reqwest::Client::new()` / `reqwest::Client::builder()` call site in the
+codebase now goes through this instead:
+
+- `auth.rs` (×3, including the one in `exchange_code()`)
+- `client.rs` (×2 — `MagisterClient::new()`, and `get_redirect_location()`'s
+  no-redirect client)
+- `ai/providers/{anthropic,openai,gemini}.rs` (×7)
+
+`Cargo.toml` gained two new explicit dependencies that `tls.rs` needs
+(`rustls`, `webpki-roots`) — both were already in the dependency tree
+transitively via reqwest, so this doesn't pull in anything new for the
+`rustls` one, and doesn't change any existing feature flags (double-checked
+reqwest's own `rustls` dependency declaration already requests full default
+features, same as before).
+
+**`Cargo.lock` will auto-update the first time you build** (plain `cargo build`,
+`pnpm tauri android build`, etc. all do this transparently). If your workflow
+uses `--locked`/`--frozen`, run `cargo build` once without that flag first
+(or `cd src-tauri && cargo update -p webpki-roots`) to refresh the lockfile.
+
+### How I verified it, without an Android toolchain in this environment
+
+I don't have an Android SDK/NDK here, so I couldn't do a full `cargo tauri
+android build`. Instead:
+
+1. Downloaded the actual `.crate` sources for `rustls-platform-verifier 0.7.0`,
+   `reqwest 0.13.4`, `rustls 0.23.43`, and `webpki-roots 1.0.9` from
+   `static.crates.io` and read the real implementations (not relying on memory) —
+   this is how I confirmed the panic site, `tls_backend_preconfigured`'s exact
+   expected type (`Option<rustls::ClientConfig>`), and `RootCertStore`'s shape.
+2. Compiled the actual `tls.rs` logic (RootCertStore + ClientConfig construction)
+   in an isolated scratch project pinned to those exact crate versions — it
+   compiles cleanly. (The reqwest-side integration point relies on the source
+   reading in step 1, since pulling reqwest itself into that scratch check hit
+   an unrelated wall: this sandbox's apt-installed Rust is too old (1.75) for a
+   transitive dependency that now requires edition2024 — that's a sandbox
+   limitation, not a real problem; your real Cargo.lock already resolves to
+   that same dependency version, so your normal toolchain will build it fine.)
+3. Traced the full call path end-to-end in the source (browser redirect → deep
+   link → `on_open_url` → `auth-callback` event → frontend listener in
+   `+layout.svelte` → `handle_auth_callback` command → `exchange_code()` →
+   the panicking `reqwest::Client::new()`) to make sure this is really the
+   only break in the chain. The frontend side looked correct — there's even a
+   comment there describing a previous, already-fixed double-invoke bug, which
+   isn't this issue.
+
+I'd still recommend actually running the login flow on a device/emulator once
+after pulling this in, since I couldn't do that step myself here.
+
+## Also noticed (not changed, your call)
+
+`[profile.release] panic = "abort"` (present in 2.1.0, absent now) — with it
+back, *any* panic anywhere in the app would immediately kill the whole process
+instead of just the one background task. I left this as-is rather than
+restoring it, since:
+
+- it's an independent decision from the actual bug (which is now fixed at the
+  source — there's no more panic in the normal login path either way), and
+- reverting it would mean any *other*, unrelated panic (e.g. in the AI
+  features) takes down the whole app instead of just failing that one
+  feature — that's a real trade-off you may want to make deliberately rather
+  than as a side effect of this fix.
+
+## Longer-term option
+
+If you'd rather use the OS-native trust store (better security posture —
+OCSP/CRL revocation checking, respects custom/enterprise CAs) instead of
+bundled roots, that means properly wiring up `rustls-platform-verifier`: add
+its Kotlin/Gradle component to `src-tauri/gen/android`, and call
+`rustls_platform_verifier::android::init_with_env` once during Android
+startup (alongside where `ndk-context` readiness is already handled, per
+`hardening-fixes.patch`/`AGENTS.md`). That's a larger, Android-build-specific
+change I didn't attempt here since I have no way to verify it compiles/links
+in this environment — happy to take a pass at it if you want to go that
+route instead.
